::GEO_U03_L04_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which criterion uses three pairs of corresponding sides?
{
=A. SSS#SSS stands for side-side-side, three corresponding side pairs.
~B. SAS#SAS uses two sides and the included angle.
~C. ASA#ASA uses two angles and the included side.
~D. HL#HL applies only to right triangles.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which criterion uses two sides and the included angle?
{
~A. SSS#SSS has three side pairs.
=B. SAS#SAS uses two corresponding sides and the angle between them.
~C. AAS#AAS uses two angles and a non-included side.
~D. AAA#AAA does not prove congruence.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which criterion uses two angles and the included side?
{
~A. AAS#AAS uses a non-included side.
~B. SAS#SAS uses two sides and the included angle.
=C. ASA#ASA uses two angles and the side between them.
~D. SSA#SSA is not a valid congruence criterion in this lesson.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which criterion uses two angles and a non-included side?
{
~A. ASA#ASA requires the side between the two angles.
~B. SSS#SSS uses three side pairs.
~C. HL#HL is for right triangles.
=D. AAS#AAS uses two angles and a side not between them.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which criterion applies only to right triangles?
{
=A. HL#HL uses the hypotenuse and one leg of right triangles.
~B. SSS#SSS can apply to any triangles.
~C. ASA#ASA can apply to any triangles.
~D. AAA#AAA does not prove congruence.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Why does AAA not prove triangle congruence?
{
~A. Angles cannot be measured.#Angles can be measured; the issue is size.
=B. Triangles can have the same angles but different sizes.#Same angles can establish shape but not size, so congruence is not guaranteed.
~C. AAA is the same as SSS.#AAA is angle information, not side information.
~D. AAA works only for right triangles.#AAA is not a right-triangle congruence criterion.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Why is SSA not accepted as a general congruence criterion here?
{
~A. It gives too many sides.#SSA gives two sides and one angle, not too many sides.
~B. It is the same as ASA.#ASA uses two angles and an included side.
=C. It may not determine a unique triangle.#SSA can create an ambiguous case, so it is not a general criterion.
~D. It always proves congruence.#SSA does not always prove congruence.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Two triangles have AB ≅ DE, BC ≅ EF, and AC ≅ DF. Which criterion applies?
{
~A. SAS#SAS would need an included angle instead of a third side.
~B. ASA#ASA uses two angles and a side.
~C. AAS#AAS uses two angles and a non-included side.
=D. SSS#Three corresponding side pairs are given, so SSS applies.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Two triangles have AB ≅ DE, AC ≅ DF, and angle A ≅ angle D. Which criterion applies?
{
=A. SAS#The congruent angle is included between the two congruent sides.
~B. SSS#Only two side pairs are given, not three.
~C. ASA#ASA requires two angle pairs.
~D. HL#HL requires right triangles with hypotenuse and leg information.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Two triangles have angle A ≅ angle D, angle B ≅ angle E, and AB ≅ DE. Which criterion applies?
{
~A. AAS#AAS would use a non-included side.
=B. ASA#The side AB is included between the two congruent angles.
~C. SAS#SAS requires two side pairs.
~D. SSS#SSS requires three side pairs.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Two triangles have angle A ≅ angle D, angle B ≅ angle E, and AC ≅ DF. Which criterion applies?
{
~A. ASA#ASA requires the included side between the two angles.
~B. SAS#SAS requires two side pairs and an included angle.
=C. AAS#The side AC is not between the two given angles, so AAS applies.
~D. HL#HL requires right triangles.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Right triangles have congruent hypotenuses and one pair of congruent legs. Which criterion applies?
{
~A. SAS#SAS is not the specific right-triangle criterion from this evidence.
~B. AAS#AAS uses two angle pairs and a side.
~C. AAA#AAA does not prove congruence.
=D. HL#HL applies to right triangles with hypotenuse and leg congruence.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which information is insufficient to prove congruence?
{
=A. Three pairs of congruent angles#AAA can leave triangles different sizes.
~B. Three pairs of congruent sides#SSS proves congruence.
~C. Two angles and included side#ASA proves congruence.
~D. Hypotenuse and leg in right triangles#HL proves congruence for right triangles.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
A diagram gives two side pairs and a non-included angle. What should you conclude?
{
~A. The criterion is SAS.#SAS requires the included angle.
=B. The evidence is not enough for a listed congruence criterion.#A non-included angle with two sides is SSA, which is not accepted here.
~C. The criterion is ASA.#ASA requires two angles and a side.
~D. The criterion is SSS.#SSS requires three side pairs.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
What does the included angle mean in SAS?
{
~A. Any angle in the triangle.#SAS needs a specific angle, not any angle.
~B. The angle opposite the longest side only.#Side length size does not define included angle.
=C. The angle between the two given sides.#The included angle is formed by the two sides named in the evidence.
~D. An exterior angle.#Exterior angles are not the included angle in SAS.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
What does the included side mean in ASA?
{
~A. Any side in the triangle.#ASA requires the specific side between the angles.
~B. The longest side only.#Length does not define included side.
~C. A side not touching either angle.#A side not touching either angle cannot be included between them.
=D. The side between the two given angles.#The included side connects the vertices of the two given angles.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
Why can SSS be connected to rigid transformations?
{
=A. Three side lengths fix the triangle size and shape for a rigid-motion match.#With all corresponding sides fixed, a rigid motion can align congruent triangles.
~B. SSS changes side lengths.#Rigid transformations preserve side lengths; they do not change them.
~C. SSS uses only appearance.#SSS uses measurements or markings, not appearance.
~D. SSS proves only similarity.#SSS is a congruence criterion.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
Why does SAS give enough evidence for congruence?
{
~A. The angle can be anywhere.#A non-included angle would be SSA, not SAS.
=B. The included angle fixes how the two sides meet.#The included angle controls the triangle formed by the two sides.
~C. Only one side is needed.#One side alone is not enough.
~D. The triangles may be different sizes.#SAS establishes same size and shape.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
Which statement correctly justifies triangle congruence by rigid motion?
{
~A. A valid criterion changes one triangle until it fits.#Rigid transformations do not change the triangle.
~B. A valid criterion ignores corresponding parts.#Corresponding parts are essential to congruence.
=C. A valid criterion gives enough evidence to map one triangle exactly onto the other.#Rigid transformations preserve size and shape and can map congruent triangles.
~D. A valid criterion relies on color.#Color is not geometric evidence.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
If two right triangles have congruent legs but no hypotenuse information, can HL be used?
{
~A. Yes, two legs are always HL.#Two legs would be a different type of evidence, not HL.
~B. Yes, because every right triangle is congruent.#Right triangles are not automatically congruent.
~C. No, because HL never works.#HL is valid when its conditions are met.
=D. No, HL needs the hypotenuse and one leg.#HL specifically requires the hypotenuse and one leg.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q21::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
Which criterion is shown by two angle pairs and a side not between them?
{
=A. AAS#AAS uses two angles and a non-included side.
~B. ASA#ASA requires the included side.
~C. SAS#SAS requires two sides.
~D. SSS#SSS requires three sides.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q22::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
Which criterion is shown by two angle pairs and the side between them?
{
~A. AAS#AAS uses a non-included side.
=B. ASA#ASA uses the included side.
~C. HL#HL is for right triangles.
~D. SSA#SSA is not a valid general congruence criterion here.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q23::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
A student chooses SAS from two sides and an angle not between them. What is the correction?
{
~A. SAS requires three sides.#Three sides would be SSS.
~B. SAS requires two angles.#Two angles would be ASA or AAS.
=C. SAS requires the included angle between the two sides.#The angle must be included for SAS.
~D. SAS is never valid.#SAS is valid when the included angle is given.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q24::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
A student chooses HL for triangles that are not marked right. What is the correction?
{
~A. HL can be used for all triangles.#HL is not a general triangle criterion.
~B. HL means any two sides.#HL specifically uses hypotenuse and leg.
~C. HL is the same as AAA.#AAA is angle information and does not prove congruence.
=D. HL requires right triangles.#HL depends on right-triangle structure.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_Q25::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
Which evidence best supports using rigid transformations to prove congruence?
{
=A. A sequence of rigid motions maps one triangle exactly onto the other.#Exact mapping by rigid motions supports congruence.
~B. The triangles have the same area only.#Equal area alone does not prove congruence.
~C. The triangles have matching colors.#Color is not geometric evidence.
~D. The triangles are drawn close together.#Location on the page does not prove congruence.
}


